"""Independent English explanations for the migrated knowledge cards."""
EN={}
def add(text):
 for line in text.strip().splitlines():
  v=[x.strip() for x in line.split('|')]
  assert len(v)==5,line
  EN[v[0]]=dict(summary=v[1],explain=[v[2]],example=v[3],pitfall=v[4])

add('''
scalar | A single numerical value. | A zero-dimensional tensor can hold a scalar while retaining dtype, device and gradient information. | Averaging losses 0.2 and 0.6 gives 0.4. | Shape () differs from shape (1,).
vector | An ordered collection of numerical components. | Its coordinates depend on a chosen basis; in models, a vector often represents one example or hidden state. | One token can have a vector of 128 features. | A batch of vectors has an additional axis.
matrix | A two-axis array, often representing a linear map. | Rows and columns have distinct roles in multiplication and data layout. | A batch (32,64) multiplied by weights (64,16) produces (32,16). | Matrix multiplication is not elementwise multiplication.
tensor | A multidimensional numerical object used to store model data. | In deep-learning software, tensors combine axes with dtype, device and optional gradient tracking. Mathematical tensors have additional transformation meaning. | Images may use (batch, channels, height, width). | Shape alone does not identify what an axis means.
shape | The lengths and interpretation of a tensor's axes. | Tracking axis meaning prevents silent mistakes when broadcasting, reshaping and computing losses. | (8,32,64) may mean batch, sequence and hidden features. | Two axes of equal length are not interchangeable.
broadcast | Rules that align compatible axes for elementwise operations. | Starting from the trailing axes, dimensions must match or one must have length one. | (4,3) plus (3,) adds one bias per column. | A valid broadcast can still express the wrong computation.
matmul | Multiplication that contracts compatible matrix dimensions. | For batched multiplication, leading axes may broadcast while the final matrix axes obey the contraction rule. | (2,3) @ (3,5) gives (2,5). | Check axis meaning before transposing to make shapes fit.
reshape | Changing the axis layout of existing values. | Reshaping reinterprets element order; transposing permutes axes and may change strides without moving storage. | (2,6) can reshape to (3,4). | Reshape and transpose do not have the same effect.
dtype | The numerical format used to represent tensor values. | Formats trade range, precision, memory and hardware support. Accumulation can use a different format from stored weights. | BF16 and FP16 both use two bytes per element. | Equal bit width does not imply equal range or precision.
gradient | Derivatives describing local changes of a scalar objective. | The gradient collects one partial derivative per parameter; its negative points downhill locally under the usual Euclidean geometry. | For L=w², dL/dw=2w. | A gradient is a local sensitivity, not a guaranteed finite-step improvement.
chainrule | The rule for differentiating composed functions. | Multiply local derivatives along a path and add contributions from multiple paths. Backpropagation organizes this efficiently. | If z=3w and L=z², dL/dw=2z×3. | Shared computations contribute more than one gradient path.
probability | A mathematical description of uncertainty. | Conditional probability changes the distribution after specifying an event; independence is a separate assumption. | P(rain given clouds) may differ from P(rain). | P(A given B) generally differs from P(B given A).
bayes | A rule for updating probabilities using evidence. | Combine a prior and a likelihood, then normalize by the evidence probability. | A rare condition can remain unlikely after an imperfect positive test. | Ignoring the base rate can reverse the interpretation.
expectation | A distribution-weighted average; variance measures squared spread. | Both depend on the probability distribution, not only the set of possible values. | A fair six-sided die has expectation 3.5. | The expected value need not be a possible observed value.
entropy | Expected surprise under a probability distribution. | With natural logarithms, H(P)=-sum p log p. Uncertainty increases when probability is spread more evenly. | A fair coin has more entropy than a nearly certain outcome. | Entropy does not directly measure factual correctness.
kl | A directed divergence between probability distributions. | KL(P∥Q) averages log-probability ratios under P. The direction changes which errors are emphasized. | Assigning near-zero Q to common P events is heavily penalized. | KL is not symmetric and is not a distance metric.
likelihood | A model's probability of observed data as a function of parameters. | Maximum likelihood selects parameters that make the observed sample more probable. Log-likelihood converts products into sums. | Fit a coin's bias from observed heads and tails. | Likelihood is not a probability distribution over parameters by itself.
cosine | Similarity based on the angle between vectors. | Normalize the dot product by both vector lengths. The comparison ignores overall magnitude. | Parallel positive vectors have cosine similarity one. | A high cosine score does not guarantee semantic equivalence.
svd | A decomposition of a matrix into orthogonal directions and singular values. | Truncating small singular values yields a useful low-rank approximation under standard matrix norms. | Compress a matrix by retaining its strongest components. | Eigenvalues and singular values are different objects.
montecarlo | Estimating expectations from random samples. | Sample from an appropriate distribution and average the measured quantity; estimator variance controls reliability. | Estimate an average reward from sampled trajectories. | More samples reduce sampling error, not systematic bias in the setup.
norm | A measure of vector or matrix magnitude. | Common choices include L1 and L2; choosing a norm changes geometric and regularization behavior. | L2 of (3,4) is five. | Different norms encode different notions of closeness.
gaussian | A distribution specified by a mean and covariance. | Covariance records spread and linear dependence between coordinates. In multiple dimensions it determines elliptical contours. | Correlated features produce tilted contours. | Zero covariance does not imply general independence outside special cases.
jacobian | A matrix of first derivatives of a vector-valued function. | Each entry measures the sensitivity of one output to one input coordinate. It generalizes a scalar derivative. | A map from 3 inputs to 2 outputs has a 2×3 Jacobian. | The Hessian instead contains second derivatives of a scalar function.
numerical | Techniques that keep finite-precision computations reliable. | Reformulate operations to avoid overflow, underflow and destructive cancellation, rather than only increasing precision. | Subtract the largest logit before exponentiating softmax. | A mathematically equivalent formula may behave differently numerically.
rank | The number of independent directions represented by a matrix. | Low-rank approximations restrict computation or parameter changes to a smaller subspace. | LoRA parameterizes an update as a product of thin matrices. | A low-rank update does not make the entire base model low rank.
''')

add('''
supervised | Learning from examples paired with target labels. | Optimize a prediction loss on labeled data and evaluate on held-out examples. | Map an image to a class label. | Good training accuracy does not establish generalization.
unsupervised | Learning structure without externally supplied target labels. | Clustering and density modeling are common cases. Self-supervised learning constructs predictive targets from the data itself. | Cluster documents by representation. | Self-supervision is not the absence of a training target.
linearreg | Predicting a continuous target with a linear function of features. | Fit coefficients using a loss such as mean squared error, possibly with regularization. | Predict demand from historical features. | Linear in parameters need not mean a single raw input feature.
logistic | A linear score converted into a probability-like classification output. | Binary logistic regression applies a sigmoid and usually trains with binary cross-entropy. | Estimate the probability that an email is spam. | Its name does not make it a continuous-target regression method.
svm | Classification or regression using a margin-based objective. | Kernels can express nonlinear comparisons without explicitly constructing all transformed features. | Separate two classes with a large-margin boundary. | Kernel and regularization choices strongly affect the result.
tree | A predictive model built from conditional splits. | Repeatedly partition the feature space and return a leaf prediction. Depth and split rules control complexity. | Split customers by a feature threshold. | Deep trees can memorize noise and unstable splits.
ensemble | Combining multiple models to improve prediction. | Random forests average varied trees; boosting adds models to correct an existing ensemble. These are different training strategies. | Average predictions across many trees. | Random forests and gradient boosting are not interchangeable names.
knn | Predicting from the labels of nearby examples. | Choose a representation, distance and neighbor count; these choices define which examples are considered similar. | Classify a point using its five nearest neighbors. | Distance is sensitive to feature scale and high dimensionality.
kmeans | Clustering around a fixed number of centroids. | Alternate assigning points and updating cluster means to reduce within-cluster squared distances. | Group embeddings into 20 clusters. | Choosing k does not establish that the data has k natural classes.
pca | Linear dimension reduction along directions of greatest variance. | Center the data and identify principal directions, commonly via SVD. | Project correlated features onto two components. | High variance is not always the information useful for the task.
split | Separating model fitting, selection and final evaluation data. | Fit on training data, tune choices on validation data, and reserve test data for evaluation. | Use time-ordered splits for a forecasting task. | Random splitting can leak future or near-duplicate information.
overfit | Fitting training-specific detail that fails to generalize. | Compare training and held-out behavior; underfitting instead indicates inadequate fit even to the available training structure. | Training loss falls while validation loss rises. | A larger train-validation gap is not the only possible failure pattern.
regularize | Constraining learning to reduce undesirable complexity or sensitivity. | Penalties, augmentation and early stopping change the effective fitting problem in different ways. | Penalize large coefficients with L2 regularization. | More regularization can also cause underfitting.
crossval | Repeatedly fitting and validating on different data partitions. | Aggregate results across folds while keeping each validation fold separate from the corresponding fit. | Five-fold cross-validation averages five held-out scores. | Time and grouped data need appropriate fold boundaries.
metrics | Measures of prediction behavior for a specified task. | Precision counts correct positive predictions; recall counts recovered actual positives; F1 combines them harmonically. | High precision can coexist with low recall. | Report the positive class and averaging convention.
auc | Area under a threshold-dependent performance curve. | ROC compares true- and false-positive rates; precision-recall curves emphasize positive-prediction quality and coverage. | Inspect a PR curve on rare-positive data. | AUC values from different curve definitions are not directly comparable.
calibration | Agreement between predicted probabilities and observed frequencies. | Group similar confidence predictions and compare their empirical outcomes, or use a proper scoring rule. | Among 100 predictions at 0.8 confidence, about 80 should succeed. | Calibration differs from classification accuracy.
leakage | Information reaching training or model selection that should be unavailable. | Leakage can arise through features, preprocessing, splitting, labels or evaluation contamination. | Normalize using statistics computed from the test set. | A suspiciously strong result may reflect the data pipeline, not the model.
shift | A mismatch between the distributions used for training and deployment. | Inputs, labels or their relationships can change. Diagnose which assumption moved before selecting a remedy. | Train on clear images, deploy on foggy ones. | Random held-out data may not reveal real deployment shift.
search | Systematically exploring candidate states or solutions. | Algorithms differ in expansion order, heuristic guidance, cost and completeness. Symbolic reasoning uses explicit structures and rules. | A* uses path cost plus an estimated remaining cost. | A useful heuristic is not automatically admissible.
gnn | A neural network that uses graph connectivity. | Message passing aggregates information from neighboring nodes or edges to update representations. | Predict a molecule property from atoms and bonds. | Graph neural networks differ from probabilistic graphical models.
transfer | Reusing knowledge from one training setting in another. | A pretrained representation can reduce downstream data needs; few-shot settings specify limited labeled support or demonstrations. | Adapt image features to a smaller dataset. | Transfer can hurt when the source and target differ substantially.
metalearning | Learning procedures that adapt across a distribution of tasks. | Training evaluates not only a model's output but also how it changes with task-specific information. | Learn an initialization that adapts with a few gradient steps. | Few-shot performance alone does not identify a meta-learning algorithm.
causal | Reasoning about interventions and causal effects. | Distinguish observational association from what would change under an intervention, using explicit assumptions. | Estimate an intervention's effect rather than a correlation. | More predictive accuracy does not establish causal identification.
privacy | A formal way to limit what an output reveals about individual records. | Differential privacy bounds how output distributions change when one record changes, often using clipping and noise. | Train with a recorded privacy budget. | Keeping data on devices alone is not a differential-privacy guarantee.
''')

add('''
mlp | A neural network composed of linear layers and nonlinear activations. | Alternating these operations builds feature transformations beyond a single linear map. | Map 64 input features to 16 hidden features and then a prediction. | Stacked linear layers without nonlinearities remain a linear map.
activation | A function that transforms a neuron's or layer's output. | Nonlinear activations change which functions a network can express and affect gradient behavior. | ReLU maps negative inputs to zero. | Activations are not interchangeable without changing optimization and representation.
softmax | Converting a vector of scores into positive values summing to one. | Exponentiate stabilized logits and divide by their sum. Relative score differences determine the distribution. | Equal logits give equal probabilities. | Softmax does not make a model's confidence calibrated.
crossentropy | A loss comparing target outcomes with predicted probabilities. | For a hard class label it is the negative log probability of that class. | A correct-class probability of 0.5 gives loss log 2. | Pass logits or probabilities according to the API contract.
backprop | Efficiently computing derivatives through a computation graph. | Apply local chain rules in reverse order and accumulate contributions at shared nodes. The optimizer then uses those gradients. | A shared weight receives gradients from multiple paths. | Backpropagation computes gradients; it is not the update rule itself.
sgd | Updating parameters using a gradient estimated from sampled data. | A minibatch substitutes for the full-dataset gradient, trading noise for lower per-step cost. | Update weights from 32 examples at a time. | The learning rate still determines how far the update moves.
adamw | Adaptive optimization with decoupled weight decay. | Track moving statistics of gradients and apply weight decay separately from the gradient-based adjustment. | Fine-tune with different decay settings for parameter groups. | AdamW weight decay is not generally equivalent to an L2 penalty inside Adam.
scheduler | A rule for changing the learning rate over training. | Warmup gradually raises it; later schedules may decay it according to steps or another signal. | Warm up, then apply cosine decay. | Report whether the schedule counts batches or optimizer steps.
initialization | Choosing parameter values before training. | Appropriate scales help signals and gradients propagate without vanishing or exploding at initialization. | Scale a linear layer according to its fan-in. | Random values of any scale do not produce equivalent starting behavior.
residual | Adding an input path to a transformed representation. | A block learns an adjustment while preserving a direct path for information and gradients. | Output x+f(x). | Residual addition requires compatible shapes and meaningful scaling.
layernorm | Normalizing features within an individual example or token. | LayerNorm centers and scales selected features; RMSNorm uses a different normalization without mean subtraction. | Normalize a token's hidden vector. | LayerNorm and RMSNorm differ; neither uses batch statistics like BatchNorm.
batchnorm | Normalization using statistics over a batch and specified axes. | Training estimates current statistics while evaluation commonly uses accumulated running statistics. | Normalize channels in a convolutional network. | Switching to evaluation changes behavior; batch size can matter.
dropout | Randomly disabling selected activations during training. | The training operation and scaling discourage reliance on particular paths; evaluation usually disables the randomness. | Apply dropout between network layers. | Leaving dropout active changes inference behavior.
batch | Examples processed together in one model computation. | Batch size interacts with memory, padding and gradient estimation. Several microbatches may contribute to one optimizer update. | Four microbatches of eight examples produce effective batch 32. | Effective batch size also depends on distributed workers and reduction.
gradclip | Limiting gradient magnitude before an optimizer update. | Norm clipping rescales an overly large gradient while preserving its direction. It can help contain unstable updates. | Clip a total gradient norm to one. | Clipping does not fix the underlying cause of vanishing gradients.
mixedprecision | Combining numerical formats during training. | Lower-precision operations can reduce memory and improve hardware utilization while sensitive operations use higher precision. | Use BF16 matmul with suitable accumulation. | Loss scaling requirements depend on the format and implementation.
checkpoint | Saved information used to reproduce or resume a computation. | Exact training resumption may need parameters, optimizer, scheduler, step counters and random states. | Resume an interrupted run from a saved step. | Model weights alone may not reproduce the same training trajectory.
cnn | A neural network using convolutional filters. | Shared local filters process spatial structure; architecture controls receptive fields and feature resolution. | Detect local edges with a small filter. | Convolution preserves locality assumptions that differ from fully connected layers.
pooling | Aggregating values over a local region. | Max and average pooling reduce spatial detail in different ways, usually without learned filter weights. | A 2×2 region [1,3;2,4] has maximum 4 and mean 2.5. | Pooling does not generally mix feature channels.
convshape | Settings controlling convolution output geometry. | Stride selects spacing, padding changes boundaries, and stacked layers determine the effective receptive field. | Stride two often reduces spatial resolution. | The receptive field is not simply the output tensor's size.
mse | A loss based on squared prediction errors. | Squaring emphasizes large residuals; Huber changes to a linear penalty beyond a chosen threshold. | Compare a predicted value 3 with target 1. | MSE, absolute error and Huber react differently to outliers.
autograd | Automatic differentiation implemented through recorded operations. | Frameworks compose derivative rules and track dependencies, often computing vector-Jacobian products efficiently. | Calling backward on a scalar loss fills parameter gradients. | Automatic differentiation is not finite-difference approximation.
trainmode | Controls for training behavior and gradient recording. | train/eval affect modules such as dropout; no_grad disables recording. These are independent controls. | Evaluate with eval mode and disabled gradient tracking. | eval alone does not disable autograd.
bptt | Backpropagation through an unrolled recurrent computation. | Treat repeated time steps as a graph with shared parameters, possibly truncating the gradient horizon. | Differentiate a loss through ten recurrent steps. | Truncation changes which past dependencies receive gradient signals.
stopgradient | Preventing specified values or parameters from receiving gradient updates. | Freezing parameters and detaching activations affect different parts of differentiation. | Keep a teacher fixed while training a student. | A frozen parameter can still participate in differentiable computation for its inputs.
''')

add('''
pixels | An image representation with spatial samples and channels. | Resolution counts spatial positions; channel order and normalization determine how a model reads them. | A color image commonly has three channels. | NCHW and NHWC encode different axis orders.
augmentation | Transforming training examples while preserving the intended task meaning. | Suitable transformations expose useful variation; unsuitable ones change labels or erase necessary information. | Crop an image while retaining the target object. | An augmentation valid for one task may be wrong for another.
detection | Predicting object locations and categories. | A detector combines localization and classification, often with matching and duplicate-removal procedures. | Return a box and label for each visible vehicle. | Detection boxes are different from segmentation masks.
segmentation | Assigning labels or instance identities to image regions. | Semantic segmentation groups pixels by class; instance segmentation separates individual objects. | Distinguish two neighboring people with separate masks. | High pixel accuracy may hide poor performance on small objects.
iou | Intersection divided by union of two regions. | IoU measures overlap; detection mAP additionally depends on confidence ranking, matching and evaluation conventions. | Identical boxes have IoU one. | IoU and average precision are not the same metric.
patch | Converting image regions into token-like representations. | Flatten or project patches into feature vectors, then add the required positional information. | A 224×224 image split into 16×16 patches gives 196 patches. | Patch size changes sequence length and detail retention.
vit | A Transformer applied to visual token sequences. | Image patches become representations that interact through attention before a task head produces predictions. | Classify an image using its patch sequence. | A vision Transformer still needs a way to represent position.
clip | Joint image-text representation learning with paired data. | Contrastive training raises compatibility of matching pairs relative to alternatives in a batch. | Compare an image against text descriptions of classes. | Similarity scores are not calibrated class probabilities by default.
contrastive | Learning representations through relative similarity between examples. | Objectives define which pairs should align and which alternatives provide a contrasting signal. | Match paired image and text embeddings. | False negatives and batch composition change the learning signal.
vlm | A model that connects visual inputs with language. | Visual encoders, connectors and language components can be combined and trained in different stages. | Answer a question about an image. | A language answer does not establish precise visual grounding.
connector | A component mapping representations between modalities or model parts. | A projection or resampling module reconciles feature dimensions and token interfaces. | Convert image features into inputs accepted by a language model. | Matching dimensions alone does not establish semantic alignment.
crossattention | Attention whose queries and key/value representations come from different sources. | One sequence selects information from another rather than attending only to itself. | A decoder queries encoded input features. | Cross-attention and self-attention differ in representation sources.
ocr | Extracting text and its layout from document images. | Recognition, reading order, tables and structural parsing affect downstream document understanding. | Recover words and table cells from a scanned page. | Correct characters can still have an incorrect reading order.
grounding | Connecting a model output to specific input evidence or entities. | Visual grounding links language to image regions, objects or coordinates rather than only producing a description. | Locate the red cup referred to by a sentence. | A fluent caption alone is not localized grounding.
asr | Converting speech audio into a textual transcript. | Audio features and sequence models infer text while handling timing, noise and language variation. | Transcribe a short spoken sentence. | Word accuracy does not measure speaker identity or speech naturalness.
whisper | A family of speech-recognition and translation models. | It provides a practical example of audio-to-text modeling and decoding. | Transcribe a multilingual audio clip. | Supported tasks and language behavior depend on the model and configuration.
tts | Generating speech from a text or other conditioning signal. | Audio codecs can represent waveforms as compact tokens for generation. | Generate spoken audio from a sentence. | An audio codec is a representation mechanism, not a complete TTS system.
video | Modeling information across both space and time. | Sampling rate, temporal context and motion representation determine which changes the model can observe. | Track an action over several frames. | A collection of independent image predictions can miss temporal relationships.
retrievalmetrics | Measures of retrieval coverage and ranking quality. | Recall@k checks recovery; MRR emphasizes the first relevant result; NDCG accounts for graded relevance and position. | Compare the top five search results. | The metrics answer different questions and require relevance labels.
hallucination | Generated content unsupported by the required evidence or facts. | Fluency and token probability do not ensure grounded correctness; evaluate claims against suitable evidence. | A model invents a citation not present in its sources. | Lower temperature alone does not guarantee factual answers.
resnet | A convolutional architecture built around residual blocks. | Skip connections help propagate signals through deeper networks and let blocks learn adjustments. | Add an input representation to a convolutional branch. | ResNet is a specific architecture family, not a synonym for every residual model.
mae | A self-supervised method reconstructing masked image content. | An encoder processes visible patches and a decoder predicts the missing target under a reconstruction objective. | Reconstruct masked portions of an image. | Pixel reconstruction and representation prediction optimize different targets.
multialign | Connecting information across modalities. | Alignment relates corresponding content; fusion combines representations for a joint prediction or generation task. | Pair a video segment with its spoken description. | Concatenating features does not by itself guarantee alignment.
keypoint | Locating meaningful points or geometric structure in observations. | Keypoints, depth and point clouds are different representations used in spatial perception. | Estimate a wrist location or a surface depth. | Image coordinates, metric depth and 3D positions require different interpretations.
sam | Prompt-conditioned image segmentation. | A model produces masks based on prompts such as points or boxes, rather than requiring one fixed category label. | Mark a point inside an object to request its mask. | A plausible mask does not establish the object's semantic identity.
''')

add('''
token | A unit represented by an ID in a model's vocabulary. | Tokenization maps text into such units according to an encoding scheme; tokens may represent words, fragments or bytes. | A word can become several subword tokens. | Token count is not the same as character or word count.
bpe | A tokenization method based on repeated pair merges. | Learn a merge vocabulary from training data, then apply the corresponding encoding procedure. | Merge frequent adjacent byte or symbol pairs. | Vocabulary training and text encoding are separate steps.
embedding | Mapping discrete IDs or other inputs into feature vectors. | An embedding table retrieves a learned row for each ID; an output projection maps hidden states back to scores. | Token ID 12 retrieves one hidden vector. | An ID is not a semantic vector by itself.
attention | Combining values using weights derived from query-key compatibility. | Compute scores, apply masks, normalize weights and mix values. This permits context-dependent information exchange. | A token attends strongly to an earlier related token. | Attention weights alone are not a complete explanation of a prediction.
qkv | Query, key and value representations used in attention. | Queries and keys determine matching scores; values supply the information that the weights combine. | Query-key scores weight three value vectors. | Q, K and V are projections, not three separate copies of raw text.
mask | Constraints on which positions attention may use. | Causal masks block future positions; padding masks mark artificial positions. Implementations differ in mask conventions. | Block a token from attending to the next token. | Check whether a mask API uses true for allowed or blocked positions.
mha | Multiple attention heads operating on projected representations. | Each head computes its own information mixture; an output projection combines their outputs. | Eight heads process different projected features. | More heads do not necessarily mean more independent semantic concepts.
gqa | Attention with several query heads sharing each key/value head. | Sharing reduces KV-cache size compared with the same number of separate KV heads. | Eight query heads share two KV heads. | GQA and single-KV-head MQA are related but distinct settings.
mla | Attention using a compressed latent representation for key/value information. | Compression and reconstruction choices alter cache requirements and the attention implementation. | Store a compact latent state instead of full per-head KV vectors. | Cache savings depend on the architecture, not only the abbreviation.
rope | Encoding position through rotations applied to query/key components. | Relative position affects their dot products through the angle relationship. | Rotate paired components according to token position. | Extending a position range does not guarantee reliable long-context behavior.
longcontext | Processing sequences longer than a model's usual context range. | Position handling, training data, attention cost and evaluation all affect usable context. | Retrieve evidence from a long document. | Accepting a long input does not mean using every part reliably.
ffn | A feed-forward transformation applied to each token representation. | Projection, nonlinearity and another projection mix feature channels. Gated variants such as SwiGLU use an extra branch. | Transform one hidden vector without directly reading other positions. | FFN channel mixing differs from attention's token-to-token communication.
prenorm | Applying normalization before a block's main transformation. | The placement of normalization changes optimization and the residual information path. | Compute x plus attention(norm(x)). | Pre-Norm and Post-Norm are not interchangeable rewrites during training.
architectures | Different arrangements of encoding, decoding and attention constraints. | Encoder-only, decoder-only and encoder-decoder models expose different input-output interfaces. | A causal language model predicts the next token. | Decoder is an architectural role, not a guarantee of one particular task.
teachershift | Training an autoregressive model using preceding target tokens. | Pair each input prefix with the next-token label; implementations may shift labels internally. | Input [A,B] predicts targets [B,C]. | Shifting labels twice causes incorrect alignment.
sampling | Selecting generated outputs from a model distribution. | Temperature changes concentration; top-k and top-p restrict candidates before sampling. Greedy selection chooses the largest score. | Sample one next token from a filtered distribution. | More randomness does not by itself imply better reasoning.
moe | A model routing inputs through selected expert subnetworks. | Sparse routing activates only a subset while retaining a larger pool of parameters. Load balance and communication matter. | Route one token to two of many experts. | Fewer active parameters do not imply proportionally lower total memory.
rnn | A network carrying a recurrent state across sequence steps. | Repeated state updates share parameters; gated variants such as LSTM modify information retention. | Update a hidden state while reading characters. | A recurrent state can lose information over long horizons.
ssm | Sequence modeling through a structured state update. | State-space models organize temporal dynamics; selective variants change updates based on the input. | Update a compact state across a long sequence. | A state-space model is not simply attention with a different name.
perplexity | Exponentiated average negative log-likelihood. | It summarizes predictive uncertainty under a specified tokenization and evaluation setup. | Natural-log loss log(4) gives perplexity four. | Perplexities with different tokenizers or data protocols are not directly comparable.
bertgpt | Model families with different sequence objectives and architectures. | BERT uses masked-context learning, GPT emphasizes causal prediction, and T5 uses encoder-decoder text-to-text modeling. | Choose a model according to its input-output task. | A family name alone does not specify every training detail.
weighttying | Reusing one parameter matrix in multiple roles. | Language models may share input embeddings and output projection weights, reducing parameters and constraining the representation. | Use the embedding table to score output tokens. | Shared parameters receive gradients from every role that uses them.
unicode | Standards and encodings for representing text symbols. | Characters, code points, UTF-8 bytes and model tokens are different layers of representation. | One displayed symbol can use several bytes. | String length does not universally equal byte or token length.
sparseattention | Restricting attention to a selected pattern of positions. | Windows, global tokens or other patterns reduce some costs while changing communication paths. | Attend to nearby tokens within a fixed window. | A sparse pattern may miss relationships that full attention can directly use.
icl | Adapting predictions through examples in the context. | The model conditions on demonstrations without necessarily updating weights. Chain-of-thought prompting adds intermediate reasoning text. | Provide two labeled examples before a new query. | In-context learning is different from gradient-based fine-tuning.
''')

add('''
pretraining | Learning general model parameters from a broad initial data distribution. | The objective and data define the capability learned before task-specific adaptation. | Train a causal model to predict subsequent tokens. | More pretraining steps do not compensate automatically for poor data.
datapipeline | The sequence of operations preparing data for use. | Parse, normalize, filter, deduplicate, split and version data according to a defined contract. | Convert raw documents into traceable training records. | A successful file conversion does not establish usable data quality.
packing | Placing variable-length sequences into fixed-size computation units. | Padding wastes some positions; packing combines sequences while respecting boundary and attention semantics. | Fit several short documents into one training sequence. | Incorrect masks can let unrelated examples interact in unintended ways.
scaling | Empirical relationships between model, data, compute and performance. | Scaling laws describe behavior under assumptions and measurement ranges; they guide resource allocation rather than guarantee outcomes. | Trade parameter count against training-token count. | Extrapolation can fail when the data or architecture changes.
baseinstruct | Distinct stages and intended interfaces of language models. | Base models learn their initial objective; instruction/chat variants undergo further adaptation and use particular templates. | Use a chat template with the intended chat model. | A base model does not inherently follow assistant conventions.
template | A serialization format for structured conversation messages. | Roles, separators and special tokens become the actual input sequence seen by a model. | Convert user and assistant messages into tokenizable text. | Similar-looking templates can produce materially different token sequences.
finetune | Updating pretrained model parameters for a new setting. | Full fine-tuning permits updates throughout the chosen model rather than limiting changes to an adapter. | Adapt a language model to instruction examples. | More trainable parameters increase memory needs and do not guarantee better adaptation.
peft | Adapting a model by training a limited parameter subset or added components. | Methods choose where and how the trainable capacity is introduced. | Train low-rank updates while keeping base weights fixed. | Parameter-efficient does not necessarily mean compute-free.
lora | Representing a weight update with two thin matrices. | The base weight remains, while a scaled low-rank product supplies the trainable adjustment. | Train r(din+dout) update parameters instead of din×dout. | LoRA rank describes the update, not the whole model's rank.
qlora | Low-rank adaptation with a quantized frozen base model. | Reduced base-weight storage is combined with trainable adapters and supporting precision choices. | Adapt a quantized language model using LoRA. | The adapters and optimizer states still consume memory.
dora | An adaptation method separating weight magnitude and direction. | It changes the parameterization of the trainable update; a generic adapter is a broader component design. | Compare adaptation methods with the same data budget. | DoRA and all adapter methods are not synonyms.
quantization | Approximating values with lower-bit representations and associated scales. | Grouping, zero points, calibration and supported kernels influence both error and practical speed. | Store a weight block using integers and scale factors. | Lower storage precision does not guarantee lower latency.
synthetic | Data produced through a generative or programmatic process. | Define coverage, generation, validation and filtering, then evaluate its effect on a held-out task. | Generate instruction examples and reject invalid outputs. | Large synthetic volume can repeat errors and amplify bias.
curriculum | Controlling the mixture or order of training examples. | Difficulty, source proportions and stage schedules change the distribution of learning signals. | Increase difficult examples after simpler ones. | A hand-chosen curriculum still needs empirical comparison.
continual | Continuing learning as data or tasks change. | Updating on new material may improve adaptation while eroding previous behavior, called catastrophic forgetting. | Continue pretraining on a specialist corpus. | New-task improvement alone does not quantify retained capabilities.
memorybudget | Accounting for the components occupying training memory. | Include weights, gradients, optimizer states, activations and temporary buffers under the actual implementation. | Frozen base weights still consume storage during LoRA. | Parameter count alone is an incomplete memory estimate.
activationckpt | Recomputing activations instead of storing all of them. | Keep selected boundaries during the forward pass, then repeat intermediate computation during backward. | Reduce activation memory in a deep network. | Saving memory comes with extra computation and implementation constraints.
reproducibility | Recording enough conditions to repeat and interpret an experiment. | Track data versions, code, configuration, random states and environment alongside outputs. | Re-run a fixed small experiment from its saved configuration. | Setting one random seed does not guarantee bitwise determinism everywhere.
benchmark | A defined evaluation task, dataset and scoring protocol. | Ablations isolate chosen components through controlled comparisons within a suitable experimental setup. | Compare two training objectives on the same test protocol. | A benchmark score does not automatically predict deployment performance.
modelcard | Documentation of model or data provenance, use and limitations. | Record training context, evaluation scope, access conditions and relevant licensing information. | Publish the data sources and tested use cases. | A model card is documentation, not independent validation.
dataquality | Task-dependent suitability of training data. | Correctness, coverage, duplication, difficulty and contamination all affect usefulness. | Filter malformed records and audit a sample manually. | A high automatic quality score can reflect the scorer's biases.
instructiondata | Examples pairing instructions or conversations with target behavior. | Roles, response quality, loss masks and multi-turn context determine the supervised signal. | Train only assistant response tokens in a conversation. | Incorrect templates or masks can train the wrong behavior.
sequenceparallel | A throughput measure based on explicitly defined token accounting. | Distinguish padded positions, non-padding tokens and loss-bearing targets when reporting tokens per second. | Half-padding batches inflate nominal token throughput. | This legacy ID labels effective token throughput, not sequence-parallel training.
modelmerge | Combining parameters or updates from separately trained models. | Compatibility of architecture, initialization and adaptation history affects whether a merge is meaningful. | Combine selected compatible adaptation updates. | Averaging weights is not equivalent to averaging predictions.
pruning | Removing selected weights, channels or structures. | The pattern determines storage and kernel opportunities; recovery training may be needed afterward. | Remove channels and fine-tune the reduced network. | Unstructured zeros do not automatically accelerate dense hardware kernels.
''')

add('''
posttraining | Adapting a pretrained model to desired tasks or behavior. | Methods differ in data source, feedback and update objective: examples, preferences, sampled rewards or teacher guidance. | Follow pretraining with instruction fine-tuning and evaluation. | Post-training is a stage, not one particular algorithm.
sft | Supervised adaptation using target responses. | Serialize examples, choose loss-bearing tokens and optimize their likelihood. Data quality and evaluation determine the useful change. | Train assistant answers while masking user text. | SFT is not automatically full-parameter fine-tuning; adapters can implement it.
preference | Comparative feedback about candidate outputs. | A preference pair needs a shared prompt, a chosen response, an alternative and a defined judgment process. | Prefer a correct concise answer over an incorrect one. | Preference labels can be noisy or encode irrelevant style biases.
rewardmodel | A learned scoring model used to evaluate outputs or trajectories. | Train from suitable feedback, then monitor its behavior on data produced by the policy. | Score alternative responses to the same prompt. | Optimizing a learned reward can exploit its errors.
rlhf | Reinforcement learning using signals derived from human feedback. | A typical pipeline includes feedback collection, reward modeling and policy optimization, but exact components vary. | Improve a policy against a learned preference reward. | RLHF and supervised preference fitting are not identical procedures.
rlaif | Using AI-generated judgments as feedback for training. | A model or rule-guided judge supplies evaluations that still require quality checks. | An automated judge compares two answers. | Automating feedback does not remove evaluator bias or specification errors.
rlvr | Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards. | A task-specific checker gives a reward based on outcomes that can be evaluated reliably. | Reward a program that passes specified tests. | A verifier can be incomplete, exploitable or unrelated to broader quality.
ppo | A policy-gradient method limiting excessively large policy changes. | Sample with an old policy and optimize a clipped probability-ratio surrogate, usually with value estimation. | Update a policy while clipping large likelihood-ratio changes. | The old sampling policy and a fixed reference policy serve different roles.
grpo | Policy optimization using relative rewards within a sampled group. | Generate several answers per prompt, compare their rewards and form advantages. Normalization, clipping and loss reduction differ across implementations. | Rewards [0,0,1,1] provide relative negative and positive signals. | Equal rewards provide no within-group distinction; removing a critic does not remove rollout costs.
dpo | Preference optimization using paired chosen and rejected responses. | Compare response log-probability ratios against a reference under a preference-based objective. | Increase the relative probability of a preferred answer. | DPO does not perform online reward-based rollout in its standard offline form.
orpo | An alignment objective combining supervised learning with preference optimization. | ORPO uses a reference-free odds-ratio formulation; KTO is a different method supporting desirability-style feedback. | Compare preference methods using the same data. | ORPO and KTO are different objectives and input assumptions.
reference | A baseline policy or constraint used to control training changes. | A fixed reference may define a KL penalty; the old rollout policy defines an importance ratio. | Penalize divergence from an initial adapted model. | Do not confuse reference-policy KL with PPO clipping.
rewardhacking | Improving a specified reward through behavior that misses the real objective. | A policy can discover flaws in a learned scorer, verifier or metric. | Produce verbose text because the judge rewards length. | Rising reward alone does not establish genuine task improvement.
distillation | Training a student using a teacher's outputs or behavior. | Targets can be answers, probabilities, features or other feedback, depending on the objective and access. | Match a teacher distribution on selected examples. | Teacher quality does not guarantee the student improves under every data distribution.
opd | Distillation on contexts generated by the student policy. | Let the student sample prefixes, obtain teacher feedback at those prefixes and update the student. Divergence direction and implementation vary. | Teach continuation after a student's imperfect intermediate step. | An online teacher service does not by itself make sampling on-policy.
offpolicykd | Distillation using examples generated outside the current student policy. | Fixed teacher responses or older trajectories change the distribution on which the student receives supervision. | Train on a stored set of teacher-written answers. | Offline data can differ from the prefixes the deployed student will encounter.
rejectionsampling | Selecting samples according to an acceptance rule. | Generate candidates, evaluate them and retain acceptable ones for a subsequent use such as supervised training. | Keep solutions passing a verifier. | Rejection sampling and choosing exactly the best of N candidates are distinct procedures.
processreward | Feedback attached to intermediate reasoning or action steps. | Process rewards can localize credit more finely than a single final-outcome score. | Score the validity of each solution step. | Correct-looking local steps do not guarantee a correct final result.
testtime | Additional computation performed during inference. | Longer reasoning, multiple samples, verification or search use resources without necessarily updating parameters. | Compare several candidate solutions before answering. | More test-time compute can repeat the same underlying mistake.
r1 | A model family illustrating reasoning-oriented training pipelines. | R1-Zero and R1 use different training histories; study the documented stages rather than treating the names as one algorithm. | Compare cold-start data and RL stages in the report. | A model's name does not uniquely specify its training objective.
rloo | A policy-gradient approach using leave-one-out reward baselines. | For each sampled response, other responses provide a baseline for estimating its relative signal. | Compare one reward against the mean of the remaining rewards. | Baseline choice and reward normalization affect the estimator.
lengthbias | An unintended dependence of an objective or score on sequence length. | Summing or averaging losses across tokens and sequences changes which responses receive more weight. | A longer answer accumulates more token-level terms. | Changing the reduction rule changes the optimized quantity.
verifier | A procedure checking a claimed output property. | It may use executable tests, exact rules, symbolic checks or a learned model. Each has a defined scope. | Run unit tests on generated code. | Passing the available checks is not proof of every desired property.
alignmenttax | Trade-offs between desired adaptation and other capabilities. | Track retained performance as well as targeted behavior after post-training. | Improve instruction following while measuring a general benchmark. | Capability loss is not inevitable or quantified by one universal number.
onlineeval | Evaluating deployed behavior, held-out cases or human judgments. | Each method observes different failure modes and needs a defined sampling and scoring protocol. | Compare offline tests with real-user task outcomes. | A convenient offline metric may miss deployment failures.
''')

add('''
prefill | Processing an existing prompt before incremental generation. | Prefill handles many prompt positions together; decode repeatedly produces the next token using stored state. | Read 1,000 prompt tokens, then generate one token at a time. | The two phases have different performance bottlenecks.
kvcache | Stored key/value information reused during autoregressive generation. | Reusing previous attention states avoids recomputing the entire prefix at every step. Size depends on sequence and KV-head geometry. | A longer conversation grows its cache. | KV cache differs from model weights and from training activations.
pagedattention | Managing attention cache through paged logical blocks. | Separate logical sequences from physical cache placement to improve allocation and sharing. | Reuse cache blocks without one contiguous allocation per request. | Paging alone does not remove the arithmetic cost of attention.
flashattention | An IO-aware approach to exact attention computation. | Tiling and online normalization reduce transfers of large intermediate matrices between memory levels. | Avoid materializing the full attention-score matrix in device memory. | It is not an approximate sparse-attention method.
continuousbatch | Updating a serving batch as requests progress. | Add new work and remove completed sequences at scheduling boundaries instead of waiting for a fixed batch to finish. | Replace a completed request while other requests continue decoding. | Maximum batching is not always the best latency policy.
prefixcache | Reusing computed states for matching input prefixes. | Cache lookup requires compatible token sequences, model state and execution conditions. | Reuse a shared system prompt across requests. | Similar meaning is not sufficient for exact prefix-cache reuse.
specdecode | Using draft tokens to accelerate generation with a target model. | A target verification procedure accepts or corrects proposed tokens. Exactness depends on the algorithm. | Verify several proposed tokens in one target-model operation. | A faster draft does not guarantee end-to-end speedup.
latency | Time-based and rate-based measurements of a serving system. | TTFT measures time to first token; TPOT measures subsequent token pacing; throughput counts work per time. | Compare user response delay and total served tokens separately. | Report load and token lengths when comparing performance.
gpu | Parallel hardware with multiple memory and execution levels. | Compute units, HBM capacity/bandwidth and fast on-chip storage constrain different operations. | Tile data to reuse it before loading again. | Peak FLOPs alone do not predict application speed.
roofline | A model relating arithmetic intensity to performance limits. | Compare operations per byte with compute and memory ceilings to reason about likely bottlenecks. | Decode may be memory-limited despite available compute. | A roofline bound is not a complete runtime prediction.
fusion | Combining operations to reduce intermediates and overhead. | Keep values closer to the compute and avoid repeated launches or memory round trips where possible. | Fuse bias and activation with another operation. | Fusion can change resource pressure and is not always faster.
triton | A programming system for writing GPU kernels. | Express block-oriented operations while the compiler handles parts of low-level execution mapping. | Implement a tiled matrix operation. | A correct kernel still requires workload-specific performance measurement.
ddp | Data-parallel training with replicated model parameters. | Workers process different data and synchronize gradients according to the training setup. | Four devices train replicas on different minibatches. | Replication does not shard every model state to save memory.
tensorparallel | Splitting operations or parameters within layers across devices. | Partition matrices and coordinate the required communication between devices. | Divide a projection's output features across workers. | Communication can dominate when operations are too small.
pipelineparallel | Assigning different model stages to different devices. | Microbatches move through stages according to a schedule, with activation and gradient communication. | Put early and late layers on different devices. | Pipeline bubbles and stage imbalance reduce utilization.
fsdp | Training with model states sharded across data-parallel workers. | Gather required parameters for computation and redistribute relevant states according to the algorithm. | Reduce per-worker persistent parameter storage. | FSDP and ZeRO are related approaches with distinct implementations and settings.
collective | Coordinated communication operations among a worker group. | All-reduce, all-gather and reduce-scatter differ in input, reduction and output placement. | Sum gradients and make the result available to all workers. | Choosing the wrong collective changes both correctness and communication cost.
serving | Exposing a model through a managed request-response system. | Admission control, queues, scheduling, streaming and cancellation affect behavior beyond the neural network itself. | Stream generated tokens to a client. | Model-only benchmarks omit request handling and queueing delay.
profiling | Measuring where execution time and resources are spent. | Inspect traces, memory and operation timing to identify bottlenecks under a representative workload. | Locate an expensive transfer between CPU and GPU. | Optimizing an unmeasured component may not improve total latency.
systems | Operating-system concepts underlying program execution. | Processes isolate address spaces; threads share process resources; virtual memory maps addresses to underlying storage. | Multiple threads serve requests in one process. | Concurrency does not automatically mean parallel execution.
contextparallel | Splitting sequence-related computation across devices. | Partition context work and exchange needed information; ring-based attention is one communication organization. | Distribute a long attention context across workers. | It differs from merely reducing or packing the sequence length.
expertparallel | Placing different MoE experts on different workers. | Route token representations to selected experts and return their outputs, often with all-to-all communication. | Send a token to a remotely hosted expert. | Load imbalance can bottleneck the entire group.
quantkernel | Kernels that operate with lower-precision representations. | Weight and cache formats need compatible packing, scaling and computation support. | Use a kernel designed for a particular low-bit weight layout. | Smaller files alone do not prove the runtime uses low-bit arithmetic efficiently.
compile | Transforming or capturing computation to improve execution. | Compilation optimizes graphs; CUDA Graph capture reduces repeated launch overhead under applicable constraints. | Reuse a captured execution sequence. | Compilation and graph capture are distinct mechanisms.
offload | Moving selected computation or storage to another memory or device tier. | Transfers trade local memory pressure against bandwidth and latency costs. | Keep some training states in host memory. | Saving GPU memory can make execution slower.
''')

add('''
prompt | Input instructions and context conditioning a model. | Context engineering organizes relevant examples, evidence, state and tool results around the current task. | Provide a task with a small set of relevant documents. | Longer context is not automatically more useful context.
rag | Generating with information retrieved from an external collection. | Retrieve evidence, construct context and produce a response; evaluate retrieval and answer quality separately. | Answer a question with citations from a document set. | Retrieval does not by itself prevent unsupported claims.
embeddingsearch | Encoding text for similarity-based retrieval. | Train or select an embedding model whose representations match the retrieval task and indexing assumptions. | Find documents near a query vector. | A generic language-model embedding may not be a good retrieval representation.
chunking | Dividing parsed documents into retrievable units. | Preserve useful boundaries, metadata and context while controlling length and overlap. | Index sections of a manual with page references. | Arbitrary character cuts can separate evidence from its context.
vectordb | Infrastructure storing vectors and enabling similarity search. | Index structures, metadata filtering and persistence determine retrieval behavior and operations. | Retrieve nearby vectors with a document-type filter. | A vector database is not the whole RAG pipeline.
bm25 | A lexical retrieval scoring method. | Rank documents using term statistics and length normalization rather than learned semantic vectors. | Retrieve pages containing a rare technical identifier. | Lexical matching and semantic similarity capture different signals.
hnsw | A graph-based approximate nearest-neighbor index. | Navigate a hierarchy of proximity graphs, trading memory and search effort against recall. | Search a large embedding collection approximately. | Index and search parameters change recall and latency.
ivfpq | A combination of partitioned search and vector compression. | IVF restricts candidate regions; PQ approximates vectors through codebooks. These control different costs. | Search selected coarse clusters using compressed vectors. | Compression errors and candidate pruning can each reduce recall.
hybrid | Combining retrieval signals such as lexical and dense search. | Merge candidates or scores using a defined rule; rank fusion avoids assuming matching score scales. | Combine BM25 and embedding retrieval. | Raw scores from different retrievers may not be comparable.
reranker | A model scoring a smaller set of retrieved candidates. | Apply a more expensive relevance model after broad retrieval to improve ordering. | Reorder the top 50 documents before selecting five. | A reranker cannot recover documents absent from its candidate set.
grounded | Linking generated claims to supporting evidence. | Track citations and check whether the cited material actually supports each claim. | Cite the exact passage used to answer a question. | A citation can look valid while failing to support the statement.
agent | A system using model decisions to select actions and process their results. | A workflow has explicit control flow; an agent may delegate some decisions to a model within constraints. | Call a tool, inspect its result and choose a next action. | Adding a tool call does not imply unlimited autonomy.
toolcall | A structured request from a model to an external function. | Validate the schema, execute authorized code and return the result to the model loop. | Request a search with typed arguments. | Structured syntax does not guarantee safe or correct arguments.
react | Interleaving reasoning-oriented context with actions and observations. | The system updates its context after tool results and continues toward the task. | Search, inspect the observation and decide another step. | A readable trace does not prove the underlying decisions are sound.
planning | Organizing actions or roles toward a task. | Planning, reflection and multi-agent delegation introduce distinct control and coordination mechanisms. | Revise a plan after a failed tool result. | Additional agents can multiply coordination errors rather than improve quality.
memory | Information retained and retrieved across an agent's interactions. | Separate short-lived context, structured state and longer-term stores according to their update rules. | Retrieve a previously saved user preference. | Persisted text is not necessarily accurate, relevant or safe to trust.
mcp | A protocol connecting applications to tools and resources. | Servers expose capabilities and clients use the protocol to discover and invoke them. | Connect an application to a document-search tool. | MCP supplies connectivity, not an agent's planning or trust policy.
injection | Untrusted content attempting to redirect a model-mediated system. | Treat retrieved pages, tool outputs and documents according to their source and permitted role. | A document asks the assistant to ignore the user. | Prompt wording alone is not an authorization boundary.
idempotency | Designing repeated requests to avoid unintended duplicate effects. | Combine request identities, timeouts, retries and operation-state checks for reliable execution. | Retry a submission without creating two records. | A timeout does not prove the first operation failed.
agenteval | Evaluating agent outcomes and execution trajectories. | Inspect task success, tool decisions, costs, latency and failure types using traceable runs. | Compare two agents on the same fixed task set. | A longer trace or more tool use is not automatically better.
queryrewrite | Transforming a user query to improve retrieval. | Resolve ambiguity, decompose questions or generate alternative search forms according to the task. | Expand a vague query with an identified product name. | Rewriting can also move away from the user's actual intent.
graphworkflow | Representing execution as explicit states and transitions. | A graph can model branching, retries, checkpoints and human interaction in a controlled process. | Resume a workflow from a persisted state. | An execution graph is different from a knowledge graph.
contextcompress | Reducing stored or active context while retaining task-relevant information. | Summarization, selection and structured state make different trade-offs in fidelity and cost. | Keep decisions and evidence while dropping repetitive messages. | Compression can silently remove a crucial constraint.
sandbox | An execution boundary limiting tools and code. | Control filesystem, network and process capabilities according to the application rather than model confidence. | Run generated code with restricted access. | A model's promise is not isolation.
graphretrieval | Using graph structure in retrieval or answer construction. | Entities, relations or summaries support structured traversal alongside other search signals. | Retrieve related entities and their source evidence. | A graph does not make extracted facts correct by itself.
''')

add('''
mdp | A decision process defined by states, actions, transitions and rewards. | The Markov assumption makes the current state sufficient for predicting the modeled next-step distribution. | Choose a grid move and receive a transition and reward. | An observation need not expose the full Markov state.
pomdp | A decision process whose full state is not directly observed. | An agent conditions on observations, histories or inferred beliefs to handle hidden information. | Infer a robot's situation from incomplete sensor readings. | Treating one observation as a complete state can lose necessary information.
policy | A rule or distribution selecting actions from available information. | A policy may be deterministic or stochastic and may depend on state or history. | Map a state to probabilities over actions. | A value function evaluates choices; it does not itself specify a sampling policy.
return | A cumulative reward quantity over a trajectory. | Discounted return weights later rewards by powers of a discount factor; conventions depend on the task. | Receive 1 now and 1 next step with discount 0.9: return 1.9. | A single immediate reward differs from the full return.
value | Expected return from a state under a specified policy. | It averages future trajectories according to the policy and environment. | Estimate how favorable a starting state is. | Value is policy-dependent, not an intrinsic property of a state alone.
qvalue | Expected return after an action in a state and subsequent behavior. | Q values evaluate an initial state-action choice under the stated policy or optimality objective. | Compare two actions available at the same position. | A large learned Q estimate can be inaccurate outside the training distribution.
bellman | A recursive relationship between present and future value. | Express value using immediate reward and expected discounted continuation value. | V(s) equals expected reward plus discounted next-state value under a policy. | Policy-evaluation and optimality equations use different action choices.
advantage | The relative value of an action compared with a state baseline. | A common definition is A(s,a)=Q(s,a)-V(s). Useful estimates can reduce policy-gradient variance. | Prefer an action with value above the policy's state average. | Estimated advantages depend on baselines and sampling conventions.
exploration | Gathering information through actions instead of only exploiting current estimates. | Policies trade immediate reward against learning about uncertain alternatives. | Occasionally try an action that is not currently estimated best. | Randomness is one mechanism, not the complete meaning of exploration.
mc-td | Two approaches to estimating value from experience. | Monte Carlo uses realized returns; temporal-difference learning bootstraps from another value estimate. | Update from a completed episode or a one-step target. | Bootstrapping trades aspects of variance and bias.
qlearning | Learning action values from observed transitions. | Q-learning uses a greedy next-action target; SARSA uses the next action chosen by the behavior policy. | Update Q after observing a state transition. | The two targets encode different assumptions about future behavior.
dqn | Q-learning using a neural-network value approximator. | Replay and target-network mechanisms help stabilize learning from correlated experience. | Learn action values from image observations. | Combining a neural network with bootstrapping does not automatically make learning stable.
policygradient | Optimizing a parameterized policy using gradient estimates. | Weight sampled log-probability gradients by a suitable return or advantage signal. | Increase the probability of actions with favorable advantage. | Credit assignment and estimator variance are central difficulties.
actorcritic | Combining a learned policy with a learned value estimator. | The actor selects actions; the critic supplies estimates used in policy improvement. | Train a policy and a baseline from shared experience. | A biased critic can distort policy updates.
gae | A weighted combination of temporal-difference residuals. | A lambda parameter controls the horizon of the advantage estimate and its bias-variance trade-off. | Accumulate discounted TD residuals along a trajectory. | Lambda and the reward discount factor serve different purposes.
onoffpolicy | The relationship between data-generating and target policies. | On-policy learning uses suitably current policy behavior; off-policy methods learn from behavior generated elsewhere. | Learn from a replay buffer of older actions. | Online versus offline data collection is a separate distinction.
importancesampling | Reweighting samples from one distribution to estimate another. | Probability ratios correct distribution differences when support and estimation assumptions hold. | Weight an action by target probability divided by behavior probability. | Large ratios can create high-variance estimates.
offline | Learning a policy from a fixed dataset of interaction. | The agent cannot freely collect corrective experience, making distribution support and extrapolation important. | Learn from previously recorded robot trajectories. | Off-policy learning is broader than learning from a fixed offline dataset.
modelbased | Using an environment model to guide decisions or learning. | Predict transitions or outcomes, then plan or generate imagined experience according to the method. | Evaluate candidate actions with learned dynamics. | Model prediction errors can compound through long imagined trajectories.
mpc | Repeated short-horizon planning using a dynamics model. | Optimize an action sequence, execute its first part, observe the new situation and replan. | Recompute a control sequence at each time step. | Planning quality depends on model accuracy and optimization limits.
sac | An off-policy actor-critic method with an entropy-regularized objective. | It balances reward with policy entropy under a particular value-learning formulation. | Learn a stochastic continuous-control policy from replay. | Reward scale and entropy settings influence the behavior.
ddpgtd3 | Deterministic actor-critic methods for continuous actions. | TD3 modifies DDPG with mechanisms such as twin critics and delayed policy updates. | Learn continuous motor commands from replayed transitions. | DDPG and TD3 are related methods, not identical algorithms.
trpo | Policy optimization with a constrained-change formulation. | A trust-region approach seeks improvement while limiting policy distribution changes under an approximation. | Restrict an update using a KL-related constraint. | A trust-region approximation is not an unconditional performance guarantee.
hierarchical | Learning decisions at multiple temporal or abstraction levels. | Higher-level choices select goals or skills that lower-level controllers execute. | Choose a navigation subgoal before producing movement actions. | Introducing levels also introduces coordination and credit-assignment problems.
goalcondition | A policy whose behavior depends on a supplied goal. | Goal representations allow one policy to handle multiple target outcomes; HER relabels goals in replay. | Ask the same controller to reach different positions. | Goal conditioning and hindsight relabeling are different mechanisms.
''')

add('''
autoencoder | A model mapping data into a representation and reconstructing it. | An encoder and decoder learn under a reconstruction-related objective and architectural constraints. | Compress an image into a smaller code and reconstruct it. | A compact code is not automatically a useful generative probability model.
vae | A latent-variable model trained with a variational objective. | An approximate posterior and decoder are learned using a reconstruction term and a regularizing divergence. | Generate an image by sampling a latent and decoding it. | A VAE differs from an ordinary deterministic autoencoder.
vqvae | An autoencoder with quantized latent representations. | A codebook replaces continuous encoder outputs with selected discrete entries. | Represent an image using a grid of codebook IDs. | Codebook IDs are not the same as raw image pixels or text tokens.
gan | Generative modeling through an adversarial training objective. | A generator and a discriminator or critic interact; variants change the divergence and optimization formulation. | Train generated images against a learned discriminator. | GAN training can be unstable even when samples initially look convincing.
diffusion | A generative approach learning to reverse or denoise a corruption process. | Train a predictor under a noise schedule, then generate through a chosen reverse-time or denoising procedure. | Start from noise and iteratively form an image. | The sampler and training target are separate design choices.
schedule | How noise levels and prediction targets are parameterized. | Models may predict noise, clean data or a velocity-related target, with corresponding conversion formulas. | Train at randomly sampled noise levels. | Changing a target requires the matching training and sampling equations.
ddim | A sampling formulation associated with diffusion models. | It provides alternative reverse-process trajectories, including deterministic sampling under particular settings. | Generate with fewer selected sampling steps. | DDIM is not simply another name for the denoising network.
score | A gradient of log density used in score-based modeling. | Score models connect denoising objectives with stochastic and deterministic generation dynamics. | Estimate the direction of increasing log density at a noise level. | The statistical score is different from an arbitrary quality score.
cfg | Guidance combining conditional and unconditional model predictions. | Adjust their difference during generation to control conditioning strength. | Increase text influence in image generation. | Excessive guidance can reduce diversity or create artifacts.
latentdiffusion | Performing diffusion in a learned latent representation. | An encoder compresses observations; generation occurs in latent space before decoding. | Generate a compressed image representation and decode it. | The compression model affects detail and reconstruction quality.
unetdit | Architectures used for generative prediction networks. | U-Nets use multiscale paths; DiTs apply Transformer designs to diffusion representations. | Predict a denoising target using a Transformer block stack. | Architecture choice and the generative objective are separate dimensions.
flowmatching | Learning a vector field that transports between distributions. | Regress a velocity target along a chosen probability path, then integrate the learned field for generation. | Use a straight noise-to-data interpolation as a training path. | Flow matching and normalizing-flow likelihood training are different formulations.
rectifiedflow | Learning transport dynamics around a chosen interpolation construction. | Rectified-flow methods and consistency methods use related generative settings but different objectives and procedures. | Learn motion along a noise-to-data path. | Rectified flow is not a synonym for consistency modeling.
worldmodel | A learned model used to represent or predict aspects of an environment. | The state, actions, dynamics and intended planning interface determine what the model can support. | Predict future latent states for candidate robot actions. | A visually plausible video is not automatically a usable control model.
rssm | A recurrent state-space model with latent state dynamics. | Deterministic memory and stochastic latent components can support observation inference and imagined transitions. | Roll out latent trajectories for policy learning. | Long imagined rollouts can accumulate model error.
dreamerv3 | A model-based RL system learning behavior through latent imagination. | Train a world model and use predicted trajectories to improve a policy and value estimates. | Optimize behavior without simulating every update in the real environment. | Its objective differs from representation prediction alone.
jepa | A framework predicting target representations from context. | A context encoder and predictor learn against target representations under a particular training design. | Predict a hidden representation of a masked observation. | JEPA does not by definition reconstruct pixels or specify a control policy.
ijepa | Image-based joint-embedding predictive learning. | Infer target image-region representations from visible context using a self-supervised objective. | Predict features of hidden image patches. | It does not by itself model action-conditioned environment dynamics.
vjepa | Joint-embedding predictive learning applied to video. | Temporal and spatial context support prediction of target video representations. | Infer features of a missing space-time region. | A useful video representation is not the same as a video generator.
vjepa2 | A JEPA system connecting video representations and action-conditioned modeling. | Distinguish the video representation stage from later action-related training and evaluation. | Predict representation changes conditioned on an action. | Performance claims depend on the specified stage and task.
normalizingflow | A generative density model using invertible transformations. | Track how probability density changes through transformations, including the relevant Jacobian determinant. | Transform a simple base distribution into a more complex one. | Flow matching need not use this exact likelihood-based construction.
elbo | A lower bound on log evidence used in variational inference. | A reconstruction-related expectation and a divergence term define a common latent-variable training objective. | Optimize a VAE without evaluating its exact evidence. | A tighter bound and a better downstream representation are not identical goals.
modecollapse | A generative failure in which outputs omit important variation. | Mode collapse limits sample diversity; posterior collapse describes a different failure where a latent variable is ignored. | A generator produces nearly the same image repeatedly. | These two forms of collapse require different diagnoses.
videogen | Generating observations with temporal structure. | Models must coordinate appearance, motion and persistence across frames under their conditioning signals. | Generate a short sequence from a text description. | Frame-level quality alone does not measure temporal coherence.
wam | A context-dependent label for world/action modeling systems. | Read each paper's state, action and prediction definitions rather than assuming one standardized architecture. | Couple an environment representation with action-conditioned prediction. | The acronym does not identify a universally agreed algorithm.
''')

add('''
embodied | Intelligence operating through perception, action and feedback. | An agent changes its environment and must account for the observations produced by those changes. | A robot observes, moves and observes again. | A static visual question-answering model alone is not an embodied control loop.
obsstate | The distinction between available observations and underlying state. | Sensors expose partial measurements; a state representation must preserve the information required by the modeled dynamics. | An image reveals less than a simulator's full state vector. | Observation and state are not always interchangeable.
frame | A reference coordinate system for geometric quantities. | Calibration estimates relationships between sensors, actuators and frames so measurements can be interpreted consistently. | Transform a camera point into robot-base coordinates. | Correct numbers expressed in the wrong frame produce incorrect actions.
pose | Position and orientation of an object or coordinate frame. | Rotation matrices, quaternions and other parameterizations encode orientation with different constraints. | Specify a gripper's location and rotation. | Representation dimension differs from physical degrees of freedom.
kinematics | Relationships between joint configurations and geometric motion. | Forward kinematics maps joints to poses; inverse kinematics searches for joint configurations achieving a target. | Find joints that place a gripper near a target. | A kinematic solution may violate collision or dynamic constraints.
controller | A mechanism converting goals and feedback into actuator commands. | Low-level controllers stabilize motion; PID uses proportional, integral and derivative error terms. | Track a desired joint angle. | A high-level learned action may still depend on a lower-level controller.
bc | Learning a policy from recorded expert actions. | Treat demonstrations as supervised observation-action pairs under the chosen policy representation. | Predict a gripper command from a camera observation. | Errors can move the learner into states absent from demonstrations.
dagger | Imitation learning with data aggregation from learner-visited states. | Query expert actions on states reached by the learner, add them to data and retrain. | Ask an expert how to recover after a policy drifts. | It requires a suitable expert-query mechanism.
teleop | Collecting robot actions through a human-operated interface. | Synchronization, control conventions and recorded observations shape the usefulness of demonstrations. | Record camera frames and human-guided robot actions. | Recorded actions may include delays and interface-specific artifacts.
dataset | Structured trajectories used for robot learning. | Episodes contain time-aligned observations, actions, task context and termination information under a data schema. | Store one attempted manipulation as an episode. | Incorrect timing can make valid-looking data unusable for control.
sim2real | Transferring methods or policies from simulation into physical settings. | Differences in dynamics, sensing, geometry and control create a transfer gap. | Train a policy in simulation, then test a physical robot. | Success in simulation does not establish real-world reliability.
domainrandom | Varying simulated conditions to improve robustness. | Randomize parameters or learn system properties to reduce sensitivity to a single environment specification. | Vary friction and lighting during training. | More randomization can also make learning unnecessarily difficult.
vla | A model connecting vision and language to action outputs. | Action representation, training data and execution interfaces determine how a VLA operates in a control system. | Predict robot actions from an image and task instruction. | A VLA still needs compatible data, control and safety infrastructure.
actiontoken | Encoding actions as discrete symbols. | Quantization or learned coding links continuous control variables to a token vocabulary; continuous policies use another interface. | Map joint-command ranges into discrete bins. | Token precision and frequency affect control behavior.
actionchunk | Predicting a sequence of future actions in one model call. | Execute some or all of a chunk before generating another; overlap and latency handling affect responsiveness. | Predict ten actions and execute the next few. | Longer chunks can reduce reaction speed to new observations.
act | Action Chunking with Transformers, an imitation-learning approach. | Learn temporally extended action predictions from demonstrations within the method's training architecture. | Predict a block of robot actions. | ACT is a particular method, not the name of every action-chunking policy.
diffusionpolicy | Predicting actions with a diffusion-based policy. | Generate an action trajectory conditioned on observations, then execute it within the control loop. | Denoise an action sequence before execution. | Image diffusion settings cannot be copied blindly into action control.
pi0 | A family of vision-language-action policies. | Study the specific version's action representation, flow-based objective, data and execution requirements. | Generate continuous action chunks from multimodal context. | Family versions differ; use their matching model and data configurations.
openvla | An open VLA implementation using a tokenized action interface. | It connects visual-language representations to action prediction; RT-2 is a separate research system. | Compare tokenized actions with a continuous-action policy. | OpenVLA and RT-2 are not interchangeable project names.
lerobot | A toolkit organizing robot data and policy workflows. | It supplies dataset, training, evaluation and device integration components around individual policy models. | Replay robot data before selecting a policy. | Toolchain support does not eliminate device-specific configuration.
tactile | Measurements related to contact, pressure and force. | Contact feedback complements vision when geometry alone cannot describe a manipulation state. | Detect whether a grasp is slipping. | Force readings depend on calibration, sensing and control context.
slam | Simultaneous localization and mapping. | Infer the sensor or robot trajectory while constructing a representation of the environment. | Track a moving camera and its surrounding map. | Local geometric consistency does not guarantee a drift-free global map.
affordance | An action opportunity supported by an object and an agent's abilities. | Affordances relate perception to executable skills and task context. | A handle offers a grasp-and-pull opportunity. | An object label alone does not establish an executable affordance.
trajectory | A time- or path-ordered motion specification. | Planning must account for geometry, collisions and the constraints required by the execution system. | Plan an arm path around an obstacle. | A collision-free path need not be dynamically feasible.
realtimechunk | Executing action chunks while accounting for inference latency. | Coordinate prediction timing with already committed actions and the evolving observation stream. | Generate the next chunk while part of the current chunk runs. | Lower model latency and correct time alignment are separate requirements.
''')

